# Response headers for hosts that can set them (Netlify, Cloudflare Pages).
#
# GitHub Pages ignores this file - it has no way to configure response headers -
# which is why index.html also carries a meta Content-Security-Policy. The meta
# version is the weaker of the two: `frame-ancestors` and `form-action` are
# ignored in a meta policy, so the directives that matter most live here. If the
# site is ever moved to a host that can serve headers, this file takes over and
# the meta tag becomes belt-and-braces.
#
# Keep the two in step: `tests/unit/index-html.test.ts` asserts the meta policy
# is present so it cannot be deleted silently during a redesign.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin

/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable